\caption(Every table size comes from the native checked length API. Lines connect integer-valued lengths; their slopes between integers do not define fractional source values.)
\alttext(Four native length curves show the rapidly growing unary cost for M two and progressively larger fixed remainder overhead for M four, eight and sixteen.)

> Specify the geometric source before choosing a parameter

Use the zero-based source \(P(N=n)=(1-a)a^n\), with \(0\le a<1\). Here \(a\) is
the tail ratio, not the success probability; success has probability \(1-a\).
The original decay, one half every eight values, means \(a=2^{-1/8}\). Its mean
is \(a/(1-a)\), about 11.05, rather than eight.

The quotient satisfies \(P(q\ge j)=a^{jM}\). Summing these tails gives
\(E[q]=a^M/(1-a^M)\). Within one quotient block the remainder law is
proportional to \(a^r\), so the probability of a long remainder is
\((a^c-a^M)/(1-a^M)\). Adding the unary terminator and remainder contributions
gives

$$E[\ell_M(N)]=\frac{1}{1-a^M}+b-1+
\frac{a^c-a^M}{1-a^M}
=b+\frac{a^c}{1-a^M}.$$
\alttext(The full expected length is b plus the tail ratio raised to the cutoff, divided by one minus the tail ratio raised to M.)

At \(a=0\), use the length of zero directly. The formula also covers \(M=1\) and
powers of two. The source entropy, a lower bound on the mean length of any
binary prefix code for individual source symbols, is

$$H(N)=-\log_2(1-a)-\frac{a}{1-a}\log_2 a.$$
\alttext(Entropy is minus the log of the success probability minus the mean source value times the log of the tail ratio.)

The original program renormalized only values below 200. That computes
\(E[\ell\mid N<200]\), omitting probability \(a^{200}\). The next table exposes
this approximation alongside the full expectation. The figure searches all
integer parameters 1 through 32 and separately marks the original four choices.
It is an exhaustive comparison over that displayed set, not an empirical fit or
a proof of optimality for arbitrary sources.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/golomb_geometric.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/golomb_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const double a = std::exp2(-1. / 8);
    const auto original =
        dm::algorithms::golomb_geometric_table(a, {2, 4, 8, 16}, 200);
    std::print("Tail ratio {:.12f}; success probability {:.12f}; mean {:.9f}; omitted mass {:.9g}.\n\n{}\n",
               a, 1 - a, a / (1 - a), std::pow(a, 200), original.to_md(12));
    std::vector<int> parameters(32);
    std::iota(parameters.begin(), parameters.end(), 1);
    const auto all = dm::algorithms::golomb_geometric_table(a, parameters, 200);
    const auto x = all.get_column<double>("M").values();
    const auto y = all.get_column<double>("expected_bits").values();
    const auto best = std::min_element(y.begin(), y.end()) - y.begin();
    const double entropy = -std::log2(1 - a) - a / (1 - a) * std::log2(a);
    std::print("Displayed minimum M={}, full mean {:.12f}; entropy {:.12f}; redundancy {:.12f}.\n",
               parameters[best], y[best], entropy, y[best] - entropy);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "full expectation", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(
        original.get_column<double>("M").values(),
        original.get_column<double>("expected_bits").values(),
        "original choices", {220, 38, 38}, 5).abline_h(entropy, {5, 150, 105},
                                                       1.5).title(
        "Geometric source: full expectation by parameter").x_label(
        "positive integer M").y_label("expected logical bits per symbol").y_limits(4, 16).save_svg("build/golomb_geometric.svg");
    if (parameters[best] != 8 || std::abs(y[best] - 5) > 1e-12)
        throw std::runtime_error("source comparison");
}
```
