> Framing, malformed input and selected work

A code is self-delimiting per word, but the parameter and the number of intended
words are external. `golomb_decode` treats count as an upper bound: clean
exhaustion can return fewer words, reaching count leaves the tail unread, and an
incomplete attempted word throws. Padding bytes are not automatically discarded.
The trace owns only the consumed prefix and complete actual records; no input
references survive. Unary counts include each terminator; remainder counts
measure actual payload visits. Their sum equals emitted or consumed bits,
excluding preflight, copying, allocation and machine costs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/golomb_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/golomb_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto e = dm::algorithms::golomb_encode_trace({6, 7, 8}, 3, 3);
    auto input = e.bits;
    input.push_back(true);  // incomplete extra word, deliberately unread at count 3
    const auto d = dm::algorithms::golomb_decode_trace(input, 3, 3, 3);
    std::print("| operation | complete words | unary positions | remainder positions | total positions |\n| " "--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| encode | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n| decode prefix | {} | " "{} | {} | {} |\n",
               e.words.size(), e.unary_bits, e.remainder_bits, e.bits.size(),
               d.words.size(), d.unary_bits, d.remainder_bits, d.bits.size());
    bool partial = false, quota = false, parameter = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::golomb_decode(input, 3, 4);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        partial = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::golomb_encode_trace({6, 7, 8}, 3, 2);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        quota = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::golomb_encode({}, 0);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        parameter = true;
    }
    const auto clean = dm::algorithms::golomb_decode(e.bits, 3, 99);
    auto copy = d;
    copy.words[0].value = 99;
    std::print("\nIncomplete attempted fourth word rejected: {}; record cap two rejected: {}; invalid " "parameter rejected on empty input: {}; clean exhaustion returns {} words; copy independent: " "{}; consumed prefix excludes tail: {}.\n",
               partial, quota, parameter, clean.size(), d.words[0].value == 6,
               d.bits == e.bits);
    std::print("Rice k=3 equals Golomb M=8: {}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::rice_encode({0, 1, 8, 99}, 3) ==
               dm::algorithms::golomb_encode({0, 1, 8, 99}, 8));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.bars({1, 2}, {double(d.unary_bits), double(d.remainder_bits)},
           "consumed positions", {37, 99, 235}).title("Actual decoder work: 9 unary + 5 remainder bits").x_label("1 unary including zeros; 2 truncated remainder").y_label("selected bit visits").y_limits(0, 12).save_svg("build/golomb_work.svg");
    if (!partial || !quota || !parameter || d.bits != e.bits ||
        clean.size() != 3)
        throw std::runtime_error("framing controls");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| operation | complete words | unary positions | remainder positions | total positions |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| encode | 3 | 9 | 5 | 14 |
| decode prefix | 3 | 9 | 5 | 14 |

Incomplete attempted fourth word rejected: true; record cap two rejected: true; invalid parameter rejected on empty input: true; clean exhaustion returns 3 words; copy independent: true; consumed prefix excludes tail: true.
Rice k=3 equals Golomb M=8: true.
\image(figures/golomb_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The extra one-bit tail is excluded by count three. Attempting a fourth word rejects it as truncated; complete input exhaustion at three remains valid for a larger count.)
\alttext(Two bars count nine actual unary bit visits and five remainder bit visits for the three decoded words, totaling fourteen.)

Every API validates \(M>0\), including on empty input. Negative counts are
invalid. Rice orders must produce a positive signed-int Golomb parameter,
normally \(0\le k\le30\); shifts are checked first. Logical size and
reconstructed integer overflow throw rather than wrap. The single-value append
API reserves the complete new size before changing an existing prefix.

Trace quotas default to one million whole word records; zero disables the policy
cap. Encoding checks the known complete record requirement before emission;
decoding admits each next record before attempting its bits. Quotas exclude
ordinary output, bit storage, capacities and peak RAM. Failures return no
partial trace. All functions use local state, borrow inputs only during the call
and permit independent concurrent calls; concurrent mutation of a borrowed
vector is outside the contract.

> Cost and limits

For an actual emitted/consumed word, bit work is \(\Theta(q+1+b)\), with the
zero-remainder case understood. Encoding and decoding are therefore not O(1) in
the represented value. The scalar length formula is O(1) on fixed-width native
integers. A large outlier with small \(M\) can demand an impractically long
unary output; a word-record quota does not bound that output.

The comparisons use logical `vector<bool>` positions. They define neither a byte
format nor metadata costs, and they do not measure throughput. Signed prediction
residuals need a separately specified nonnegative mapping. No general claim that
this module implements FLAC or JPEG-LS follows from sharing a coding family.

Synthetic dictionaries and a fully specified source suit this question: they
isolate code geometry and tail matching without forcing an unrelated dataset. A
parameter sweep compares the given model; a changed distribution needs a new
comparison, including side information and framing in any end-to-end compression
claim.

> Source and verification

S. W. Golomb, [Run-Length Encodings](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1966.1053907),
IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 12(3), 399–401 (1966), introduces the
geometric run-length setting and dictionaries, with especially simple matched
cases where \(a^M=1/2\). An accessible [primary-paper
scan](https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee398a/handouts/papers/Golomb%20-%20Run-Length%20Codes%20-%20IT66.pdf)
gives the encoding construction in Section II. Here the expectation and Kraft
identities are derived for the library's explicit zero-based convention; the
displayed sweep supplies its own numerical evidence.

Native tests check dictionaries against an independent binary-leaf construction,
reconstruct actual spans and work, and exhaust short bit streams for
malformed/framing behavior. Boundary, ownership, quota, signed-shift and virtual
integer-overflow cases run with sanitizers. Independent high-precision source
sums check the expectation. Report programs, figures, documentation examples and
both exports are verified before retiring the legacy report.
